  Content: "\"Earrings for Peggy (After Calder)\"\nAndew Maize\n\nThese earrings
    are modelled after a pair Alexander Calder made for art collector Peggy Guggenheim
    in 1938. Peggy Guggenheim was born in 1898, in the middle of the Klondike Gold
    Rush. Her uncle Solomon Guggenheim founded the Yukon Gold Company in 1906, and
    mining made the Guggenheim family one of the wealthiest in the world. Guggieville
    was the company headquarters in the Klondike. Solomon became a philanthropist
    and art collector, amassing a large collection of modern art, which is now the
    Guggenheim museum in New York City. Peggy\u2019s father, Benjamin Guggenheim
    died in the sinking of the Titanic.\n\nWhen Peggy turned 21, she inherited 2.5
    million US, (equivalent to US$45.3 million in 2024). She led a bohemian lifestyle,
    and moved to Paris, where she met Marcel Duchamp who introduced her to many contemporary
    artists such as Max Ernst and Alexander Calder. Inspired by her uncle, she began
    her own art collection, and she played a major role in the careers of many artists
    such as Duchamp, Calder and Jackson Pollock - three artists who have had a major
    influence on my artistic practice.\L\LSo I decided to make this pair of earrings,
    mobiles and a bicycle wheel. Even a bad dripped painting that you\u2019ll never
    see. I\u2019m not really sure why I made these things, other than as a way to
    think through the deeply intertwined and complicated relationship between place,
    cultures, resource extraction, wealth, history and our contemporary moment. That\u2019s
    a lot of things, I know. They were also shaped by the materials that I was able
    to find, forage and source from around town. For instance, if I had not found
    a roll of copper wire - I would have never made these earrings. \L\LI would like
    to thank KIAC, Steps, Peter Menzies and Matt Charbonneau for their support in
    making this piece.\n"
  RavenSucessScript: '"Ahh...so your feathers found the Shimmer! ''Earrings for Peggy'',
    a wink to Calder, and a kiss of Dawson flair. Not all reasures hang in plain
    sight! Caw"

'
